

		Mac-2-Dos Interface

		March 23, 1989

Mac-2-Dos includes a hardware interface which attaches to the Amiga
external floppy drive port or to the daisy-chain port on any
external Amiga floppy.  The interface permits the Mac drive to
function as DF1, DF2, or DF3.  The interface does not include a
daisy-chain connector, which means that this interface must, of
necessity, be the last device in the chain.  And, of course, the
Amiga allows no more than three external drives, including the
Mac-2-Dos interface.

The interface consists of four ICs and seven pull-up resistors. 
The design uses these five bits of CIAB to control the Mac drive:
_SELn (1, 2, or 3, depending upon configuration), _MTRX, _SIDE,
DIR, and _STEP.  The Mac drive uses seven control signals:
_DriveEnable, CA0, CA1, CA2, SEL, LSTRB, and MotorSpeed.  In order
to provide these signals, the _SELn lead is multiplexed to provide
_DriveEnable, LSTRB, and MotorSpeed.

The multiplexing is performed in this way.  FF1 latches ON when
_SELn is first selected.  FF1 provides _DriveEnable to the Mac
drive for the duration of the operation.  FF1 is reset only when
_SELn, CA0, CA1, CA2, and SEL are all set to 1 (Normal default for
Amiga in the quiescent state).  While FF1 is ON and FF2 is OFF, SELn
is gated produce the LSTRB signal, the write-gate for the Mac
control registers.  This permits reading from and writing to the Mac
registers, as needed for status and head positioning, prior to
starting an I/O operation.

When FF2 is ON, SELn is gated to MotorSpeed.  By toggling SELn with
the appropriate duty cycle, the speed of the Mac drive can be
controlled as necessary to read/write the selected track.  FF2 is
toggled on or off by setting CA0, CA1, CA2, and SEL while SELn is 0.
Note FF2 can be turned off without deselecting the drive so that
LSTRB can again be generated, if necessary.

FF1 and FF2 are both reset by simultaneously setting SELn, CA0, CA1,
CA2, and SEL.  This deselects the drive and returns the interface to
the initial condition.

Note that drive status information is passed from the drive on the
_ReadData signal.  Since the driver program cannot effectively read
this signal, it is gated to the _Rdy signal to the Amiga, which is
available on bit 5 of CIAA.  The _ReadData signal is also gated,
since some Mac drives pull _ReadData low even when not selected,
causing the Amiga to be unable to read from any floppy drive.  



